nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\nHI NIMI VATIED\nBeaut\nN\nKAT\nKANAB\nMEDICALLY ESPOILED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Sand Dune Arch\",\n  \"distance\": \"8 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"elevation\": \"6000 FEET\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.146567\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.585867\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"4WD\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"YES\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"NO\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"2 HOURS\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"NORTH\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 8 miles North on HWY 89. Just past milepost 71, turn left onto the Hancock Road. Drive approximately 8 miles and turn right on Road #648. Hike from here. At the point 4WD is required. Head towards a large hoodoo. Park here. Check out the view.\",\n  \"description\": \"If you are going to visit the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park, or just have a few hours to spare, you will want to check out this unique arch in the sand located off of the Hancock Road. The arch is nestled among other beautiful rock formations, pinion and juniper trees.  You can hike from the Hancock Road or if you have 4WD, you can drive right up to it.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>If you are going to visit the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park, or just have a few hours to spare, you will want to check out this unique arch in the sand located off of the Hancock Road. The arch is nestled among other beautiful rock formations, pinion and juniper trees. You can hike from the Hancock Road or if you have 4WD, you can drive right up to it.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"You will also see amazing views of the White Cliffs, one of the layers of the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads may be impassable when wet. Respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is limited in most areas. Do not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no traces of your visit so that others may enjoy.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:36:35','2023-09-22 02:43:19'),(46,'trails',301,'SMOKY MOUNTAINS\nSMOKE HOLES\n327\nNIPPLE\nBIG\nWATER\nBIG\nWATER\nVISITOR\nCENTER\n(KANAB 57 mi\n320\nROAD\n9.3 mi\nADVENTURE MAP\nA+ 000\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nSMOKY\nVEHICLE:\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nSMOKY MOUNTAIN\nHIGH CLEARANCE 4WD\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nDISCLAIMER:\nothers may enjoy.\"\nTIBBITTS\nYEAR ROUND\nHIGH CLEARANCE 4WD\nGETTING THERE:\n• From Kanch, drive $7 miles east on HWY 89.\nJust past mile pust 7, turn left onto road #300, the Smoky Mountain Road.\n• Drive 9.3 miles to the intersection of Smoky Mountain Road and Crosby Canyon and stay left.\nDrive 3.7 miles to rood #332.\n• Continue for 9.9 miles and turn right, you will arrive at the smoke holes in less than a mic\negne, you\n• Dirt roads can be impesseble when wet. Check local weather conditions.\nCANYON\n* Near Big Water, the trail runs across the steep Kelly Grode, an exciting drive with narrow switchbacks.\nA few sections require a high clearance 4-wheel drive vehicle. This is a very remote desert, with limited\nto NO cell phone service. Be prepared, have a vehicle in good working order and bring plenty of\nfood water gas and a shovel.\n326\nYES\nSHORT DISTANCE\nROAD\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check locul oud and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads ore mpassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water.\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deloce the \'rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\nFULL DAY\n300\nDIFFICULT\n4500-6500 FEET\n300 MOUNTAIN\nROAD\nThe Smoky Mountain road is the langes\nof the backcountry byways that cross\nthrough the Grand Staircase-Escalante\nNational Monnument. It got its name from\nan actual smaking mountain. Fire is burning\nin this scenic landscape and smake hos\nbeen seeping on this oncient\nunderground fire for millions of years.\nBecausn the ground is rich in ron deposits\nand most likely ignited by lightning, enough\noxygen enters surface cracks to keep the\ncoal smoldering. The distant blue of the\nlake is backdrop to a slark and barren\nlandscape here. Rolling mud hills of grey,\nbrows, gold and white host little\nvegetalion but a multitude of colors.\nAlong the route there are some beautiful\nviews of lake Powel.\nB\n3.7 mi.\nCROSBY CANYON\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nCLEVATED\n9.9 mi\nGPS 111 25.732\n37 13.601\nKANAB\nMASICALLY UNSPOILED\nHOLE\nಪ\n332\n77 mi\n340\nRETR\nUT KANAB\nAPP\nMILLY EXPL\nTorin','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Smoky Mountains\",\n  \"distance\": 327,\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Difficult\",\n  \"elevation\": \"4500-6500 Feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.227\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-111.429\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"High Clearance 4WD\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"Full Day\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"High Clearance 4WD\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 57 miles east on HWY 89. Just past mile post 7, turn left onto road #300, the Smoky Mountain Road. Drive 9.3 miles to the intersection of Smoky Mountain Road and Crosby Canyon and stay left. Drive 3.7 miles to road #332. Continue for 9.9 miles and turn right, you will arrive at the smoke holes in less than a mile.\",\n  \"description\": \"The Smoky Mountain Road is one of the backcountry byways that cross through the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. It got its name from an actual smoking mountain. Fire has been burning in this scenic landscape and smoke has been seeping from this ancient underground fire for millions of years. The ground is rich in iron deposits and most likely ignited by lightning, enough oxygen enters surface cracks to keep the coal smoldering. The distant blue of the lake is a backdrop to a stark and barren landscape here. Rolling mud hills of grey, brown, gold, and white host little vegetation but a multitude of colors. Along the route, there are some beautiful views of Lake Powell.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>The Smoky Mountain Road is one of the backcountry byways that cross through the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. It got its name from an actual smoking mountain. Fire has been burning in this scenic landscape and smoke has been seeping from this ancient underground fire for millions of years. The ground is rich in iron deposits and most likely ignited by lightning, enough oxygen enters surface cracks to keep the coal smoldering. The distant blue of the lake is a backdrop to a stark and barren landscape here. Rolling mud hills of grey, brown, gold, and white host little vegetation but a multitude of colors. Along the route, there are some beautiful views of Lake Powell.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Near Big Water, the trail runs across the steep Kelly Grade, an exciting drive with narrow switchbacks. A few sections require a high clearance 4-wheel drive vehicle. This is a very remote desert, with limited to no cell phone service. Be prepared, have a vehicle in good working order and bring plenty of food, water, gas, and a shovel.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:36:47','2023-09-22 02:43:42'),(47,'trails',302,'SOUTH FORK\nINDIAN CANYON\nPICTOGRAPHS\nN\n#7\nYELLOW\nROAD\nJACKET\nSAND\nSPRING\n3.44 mi\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES STATE PARK\n50\nBIG SAND\nDUNE\nSOUTH FORK\nINDIAN CANYON PICTOGRAPHS\n50\n2 mi\n51\nADVENTURE MAP\n00\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\n45 247\nSouth Tork Indian\nCars Raajuph\nTrad\nVEHICLE:\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nPICTOGRAPHS\nP\nWAW\n5.9 mi\n4WD ROAD\nTO SAND SPRING\nGPS 37 34.09\n112 35.256\nSTART HEDHIND\nINFORMATION SOM\nYES\nYEAR ROUND\n4WD/ATV\n1.5 MILES ROUND TRIP\nA DAY\nGETTING THERE:\n*\nFrom Kanab, toks HWY 89 north for 7,2 miles and tum left after mills post 71 onto the\nHancock Road.\n* After 5.9 miles, tum left onta dirt road 50.\nAWD le now required, as the sond te very deep.\nEASY\n* Watch for an Intersection with a sign directing you to turn east to the South Fork Indian\nCanyon Pictograph trollhead.\nThe road narrows for the last 1,8 milau.\nThe road ends at the point on the ridge.\n* Stort the hils on the well beaten trall behind the Information sign.\ntread lightly!\nDISCLAIMER\n*This map and directions ons Imended for general reference use only. Always chack local mad and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt mach are imposable when vest. Placas respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wildlemsas experience: Always bring extra water,\nfood, sumačman, and supplies. Cell phone survica la non-endetent in incal oracul\nDo not remove orffects or deince the \'rock ar?. Lenys no troca of your vall so\nothers may nalay\nKarl Mator\'s Cub: 78 $ 100 E\nPHONE: 495.644.5039\n566\nThe Balmer people painted on these wolle\nbetween 452 AD and 62 BC. The pictographs\nveses patated wing natural pige found in\nthis area lita ochina, rabbit brusk and mineral\ned\ndeposits. Becuse of the delicate notaru\nof anden painage they are usually\nfound in corsar alcove where they lo\nbeen protected from the elements.” The\nBasketmaker people lived above these canyons\nphouses. They were lamers, hunters and\ngalore. The canyon below offered an\nabundant supply of water. Start your ha\nbehind the informally sign on the wal-beaten\nthrough the sand, then gradually descend\nbeneath\n6200 FEET through roda Follow the\nHAN COCK ROAD\nthe diffband be your level you reach the\nalcore. \"Vw the wings behind the fence.\"\nLIFE\nUTAH\nELEVATED\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY DOSPOILED\nMC\n食\nMILE\n7.8 mi 7\nNORTH\n89\nPama?\nGENERATE\nUT KANAB\nMILLY PED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"South Fork Indian Canyon Pictographs\",\n  \"distance\": \"2 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"elevation\": \"6200 FEET\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.5683\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.5876\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"4WD/ATV\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"1.5\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"4WD\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, take Highway 89 north for 7.2 miles and turn left after milepost 71 onto the Hancock Road. After 5.9 miles, turn left onto dirt road 50. AWD is now required, as the sand is very deep. Watch for an intersection with a sign directing you to turn east to the South Fork Indian Canyon Pictograph trailhead. The road narrows for the last 1.8 miles. The road ends at the point on the ridge. Start the hike on the well-beaten trail behind the Information sign.\",\n  \"description\": \"The Balmer people painted on these walls between 452 AD and 62 BC. The pictographs were painted with natural pigments found in this area like ochre, rabbit brush, and mineral deposits. Because of the delicate nature of ancient painting, they are usually found in sheltered alcoves where they have been protected from the elements. The Basketmaker people lived above these canyons in pithouses. They were farmers, hunters, and gatherers. The canyon below offered an abundant supply of water.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>The Balmer people painted on these walls between 452 AD and 62 BC. The pictographs were painted with natural pigments found in this area like ochre, rabbit brush, and mineral deposits. Because of the delicate nature of ancient painting, they are usually found in sheltered alcoves where they have been protected from the elements. The Basketmaker people lived above these canyons in pithouses. They were farmers, hunters, and gatherers. The canyon below offered an abundant supply of water.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local maps and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads are impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in inaccessible areas. Do not remove or deface rock art. Leave no trace of your visit so others may enjoy.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:36:53','2023-09-22 02:44:01'),(48,'trails',303,'STAY A LITTLE LONGER\nALTERNATIVES TO THE WAVE\nBASECAMP KANAB\nWHITE POCKET\n4WD/ATV: difficult getting there\nHIKE: easy, 1 mi\nWIRE PASS\nHIKE: 2 mi, easy, one obstacle. 8ft. drop\nBUCKSKIN GUICH\nHIKE: difficult, 21 mi\nCEDAR MOUNTAIN\n-112 47.133, 37 32.637\n58 2 miles from Kanab\nALTERNATIVES\nTO THE WAVE\nPEEKABOO TRAIL\n-112 33.564, 37 10.774\n12.5 miles from Kanab\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES\n-112 43.919, 37 2.263\n26 miles from Kanab\nHWY9\nHWY14\nINCHWORM ARCH\n4WD/ATV: easy but sandy\nHIKE: easy, .25 mi\nPEEK A BOO SLOT CANYON\n4WD: hard, sandy/ATV: moderate\nHKL: easy, I mi\nSo you\'ve come to Kanab to try your luck? It\'s a good thing your not in Las Vegas, because here you have nothing to losel Even if you\ndon\'- win the Wave lottery, you\'re clready lucky to be in the heart of the greatest concentration of natural woncers possibly anywhere in\nthe world. Every morning that you don\'t win the Wave lotery, it gives you a chance to explore a nearby national park or hidden gem.\nFor more information on alternative destinations, swing by the Kane County Visitor Center 78\'S 100 E Kanab UT.\nHWY89\nTOADSTOCI\nLIKE: easy family friendly.\n2 mi round trip\nCEDAR MOUNTAIN\nMAMMOTH CAVE\nATV/HORSE/SNOWMOBILE\nHIKE: easy .25 mi\nBarnes\nADVENTURE MAP\nMAMMOTH CAVE\n-112 35.288, 37 34.094\n53 miles from Kanabi\nKANAB\nINCHWORM ARCH\n-112 18.961, 37 6.636\n30 miles from Kanab\nSKUTUMPAH ROAD\nJOHNSON CANYON\nPARIA RIVER HIKE\n111 57.291, 37 15.063\n39.4 miles from Kanab\nHWY89\na\nBUCKSKIN/WIRE PASS\n-112 1.474, 37 1.178\n46.85 Miles from Kanab\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES\nPLA