s\n5800\nموها\nKANAB\nHOLLY SPOILED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Squaw Trail\",\n  \"distance\": \"3 miles round trip\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Moderate\",\n  \"elevation\": \"5040-5802 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"\",\n  \"longitude\": \"\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"No\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Unknown\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"2-3 hours\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"Any\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"At the coplight of Cemer and ivain, take a right onto Main Street, then right on 300 north. Go 1 block and turn left on 100 E. Drive past Jacob Hamblin Park on the left and continue straight to the trailhead. Start at the Squaw Trail sign. Head north and follow the signs. See the Kane County Visitor Center for additional maps. You may also download the Kanab Trails App.\",\n  \"description\": \"Kanab is becoming well known for its hiking trails, and Squaw Trail is a favorite as it can be accessed right from town. If you only do one hike while visiting Kanab, make it this one. The trail is considered a moderately steep area with a 780 ft. elevation. There are spectacular views of the town, Arizona, and the surrounding areas. The trail has many amazing rock formations, and when you reach the top, you can connect to a whole network of other trails.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Kanab is becoming well known for its hiking trails, and Squaw Trail is a favorite as it can be accessed right from town. If you only do one hike while visiting Kanab, make it this one. The trail is considered a moderately steep area with a 780 ft. elevation. There are spectacular views of the town, Arizona, and the surrounding areas. The trail has many amazing rock formations, and when you reach the top, you can connect to a whole network of other trails.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"DISCLAIMER: This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads may become impassable when wet. Respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service may be inconsistent in most areas. Do not remove artifacts or deface the trail. Leave no traces of your visit.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:17','2023-09-22 02:44:46'),(51,'trails',306,'LOTTERY LOCATION\nThe only legal lottery in Utah\nGRIND\n89\nFLORY\nLOBAR\nWAVE LOTTERY\nORIENTATION VISITOR\nCENTER\n76 S\n1\nTOO E\n0\n0\nKANAB\nCONVENTION\nCENTER\nNORTH COYOTE BUTTES\n(The Wave)\npick up permits 6- 8:30 am\nsafety briefing 9:00 am\n89A\n• Due to the fragile nature of the area a permit is required for hiking South Coyote Buttes\nand North Coyote Buttes \"The Wave\".\nSOUTH COYOTE BUTTES\npickup permit 9:00 am\nsafety briefing 9:15 am\n• North Coyote Buttes walk-in is limited to 4 groups or 16 people which ever is drawn first.\n• South Coyote Buttes is 10 permits a day\n• Permits are drawn by online lottery at Rec.gov\n• Pick up permits at Kane County Office of Tourism /8 S. 100 E.\nKANAB\nMARICALLY UNSPOILED\n...\nPROCESS:\nDay 1: Apply at 6 am to 6pm. You will be notified by email that\'s sent out at 7:15 pm. Accept by 8 am.\nDay 2: Complete acceptance by 8 am, pick up at 8 am (Tourism Office) and 8:30 am safety briefing.\nDay 3: Hike the North or South Coyote Buttes whichever permit you won.\nVisitSouthernUtah.com 435 644-5033','{\n\"trail_name\": \"Lottery Location\",\n\"distance\": \"\",\n\"best_season\": \"\",\n\"difficulty\": \"\",\n\"elevation\": \"\",\n\"latitude\": \"\",\n\"longitude\": \"\",\n\"accessibility\": \"\",\n\"pets_allowed\": \"\",\n\"reservation_required\": \"\",\n\"average_hours_to_hike\": \"\",\n\"vehicle\": \"\",\n\"getting_there\": \"\",\n\"description\": \"\",\n\"description_html\": \"\",\n\"additional_info\": \"The only legal lottery in Utah.\\n\\nGrind: 89\\n\\nFlory: Lobar\\n\\nWave Lottery Orientation Visitor Center: 76 S\\n\\nToo E\\n\\n0\\n\\n0\\n\\nKanab Convention Center\\n\\nNorth Coyote Buttes (The Wave) - pick up permits 6 - 8:30 am, safety briefing 9:00 am\\n\\n89A\\n\\nDue to the fragile nature of the area, a permit is required for hiking South Coyote Buttes and North Coyote Buttes \\\"The Wave\\\".\\n\\nSouth Coyote Buttes - pick up permit 9:00 am, safety briefing 9:15 am\\n\\nNorth Coyote Buttes walk-in is limited to 4 groups or 16 people whichever is drawn first.\\n\\nSouth Coyote Buttes is 10 permits a day.\\n\\nPermits are drawn by online lottery at Rec.gov.\\n\\nPick up permits at Kane County Office of Tourism /8 S. 100 E.\\n\\nKanab\\n\\nMarically Unspoiled...\\n\\nProcess:\\n\\nDay 1: Apply at 6 am to 6 pm. You will be notified by email that\'s sent out at 7:15 pm. Accept by 8 am.\\n\\nDay 2: Complete acceptance by 8 am, pick up at 8 am (Tourism Office), and 8:30 am safety briefing.\\n\\nDay 3: Hike the North or South Coyote Buttes, whichever permit you won.\\n\\nVisitSouthernUtah.com 435 644-5033\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:27','2023-09-22 02:45:02'),(52,'trails',307,'TOADSTOOLS\nHIKE\nTOADSTOOLS\nHIKE\nO\nKANAB 45 mi\nx 4584\n4633\nADVENTURE MAP\n88 % 10\n496 /\nCAND\nMILE\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nEXPOSURE:\nHIKING\nELEVATION:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY: EASY FAMILY FRIENDLY\n600\nHave you ever wanted to visit another\nplanet? Well, this is the hike for you!\nYEAR ROUND Bring your camera to take pictures on\nthis other worldly, family friendly hike\nof all the alluring natural wonders\nof balancing rocks and hoodoos\nwith their unique colors and\nshapes. This hike gets its name\nfrom the toadstool formations\nas seen at the end of this hike. At\ntoadstool is a rock formation which\nhas a boulder perched on a rock of\na smaller diameter, like a mushroom,\nor \"toadstool.\" This environment is the\nperfect condition for these formations.\n2 MILES ROUND TRIP\nGPS 376.494\n112 52.254\nTOADSTOOLS\n1 mi\n4456\nFULL SUN\nGETTING THERE:\n• From Kanab drive 45 miles east on HWY 89\n• The parking lot and trailhead will be just past milepost 20 on your left.\n• The trail begins just behind the information sign.\n• Follow the wash and the well defined trail to the noodoos.\n.\n• Exploring the surrounding area, you will see many balancing rock and hoodoos.\n• This environment is an open and shaceless desert. It is extremely hot in the summer.\n• Be prepared with enough water and sunscreen.\nEAST\n89\nNO\nHALF DAY\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dit roads are impassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water,\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-ex stant in most araasl\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock art\'. Leave no trace of your visit so\nothers may enjoy\nKanal Visitor\'s Center: 78 S 100 E\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\n4456 FEET\nHE\n4655\n4302\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\n3.6.\nHAL TRANS\nAPP\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY POILED\nMR\nRIM\nTRAILHEAD\n220\nKANAB\nNega LED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Toadstools Hike\",\n  \"distance\": \"2 miles round trip\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy Family Friendly\",\n  \"elevation\": \"600 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.6494\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.8709\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"Full sun\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"No\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"Half day\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"Car\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 45 miles east on HWY 89. The parking lot and trailhead will be just past milepost 20 on your left. The trail begins just behind the information sign. Follow the wash and the well-defined trail to the hoodoos.\",\n  \"description\": \"Have you ever wanted to visit another planet? Well, this is the hike for you! Bring your camera to take stunning pictures of the alluring natural wonders of balancing rocks and hoodoos with their unique colors and shapes. The toadstool formations at the end of the hike are rock formations with boulders perched on smaller rocks, resembling mushrooms or \'toadstools\'. This hike is family-friendly and offers an otherworldly experience.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Have you ever wanted to visit another planet? Well, this is the hike for you! Bring your camera to take stunning pictures of the alluring natural wonders of balancing rocks and hoodoos with their unique colors and shapes. The toadstool formations at the end of the hike are rock formations with boulders perched on smaller rocks, resembling mushrooms or \'toadstools\'. This hike is family-friendly and offers an otherworldly experience.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Exploring the surrounding area, you will see many balancing rocks and hoodoos. This environment is an open and shadeless desert, extremely hot in the summer. Be prepared with enough water and sunscreen. Please check local road and weather conditions before visiting. Respect private property and do not remove artifacts or deface rock art. Leave no trace of your visit for others to enjoy.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:38','2023-09-22 02:45:18'),(53,'trails',308,'89\nSCENIC\nKANAB\n>60 mi\nADVENTURE MAP\nMAX\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBYWAY 12\nBEST SEASON:\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nGETTING THERE:\nTROPIC RESERVOIR\nVEHICLE:\nLOTS OF ATV TRAILS\nHIKING: LOTS OF HIKING OPPORTUNITIES\nACCOMMODATIONS:\n19 CAMP SITES\nothers may enjoy.\"\n• Fram Kanab drive 60 miles north on Scenic Highway 89.\nTum right on Scenic Byway 12. Slow down and enjoy the beauty of Red Canyonl\n• Trave about 13.5 miles, and just past mile post 10 turn right (road #87).\nAfter 7 miles you will see a sign for Tropic Reservoir unc Kings Creek Campground.\n• Tum right and continue around the reservoir for a half a mile to the parking area for the\nPansaugunt trailhead.\n• If you have time take the 3 mile AIV trail to Ruby\'s Inn for breakfast lunch or dinner.\n12\nDISCLAIMER:\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads cre mpassable when wel. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nKANAB\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILE\nPALINJAUCUNT\nRED CANYON\nTROPIC RESERVOIR\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\nDIXIE NATIONAL FOREST\nTropic Reservoir is a small scanic lake,\nsituated in the Dixie National Forest of\nSouthern Utah. It is very popular lor fishing,\nboating, ATV riding, mountain biking, hiking.\nand snowmobiling. The Pounsagaunt Trail\nhus views of the \'Fink Cliffs on the\nGrand Staircase National Forest\nand Keibah Plamau. Kings Cmak\nCampground has a paved boct ramp,\nsurable for small bacts and kayaks. Fishing\nfor brown, brook, rainbow and cuthreat\ntrou is great from the shore or boo. An\nATV ride west from the compground und\nover the beautiful mountain will get you to\nover the\nHatch. Riding the trails south and post will\ntake you to spectacular orange hoodoos\nEASY\n7835 FEET just outsice Bryce Canyon, with breathtaking\nscenery and excellert hiking opportunities.\n18 ml\nMAY-SEPTEMBER\nATV LOOP\nNO\n13.5 mi\nMILE\nEAST\nX\nTROPIC\nRESERVOIR\n7 mi\nISTE\n# 87\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\n2.8 mi\n(12)\nAT TRAIL TO RUBY\'S INN\nBRYCE\nCANYON\nUN N\nUT\nAPP\n2.6 mi\nRUBY\'S INN\nNATIONAL\nPARK\nN\nKANAB\nMISPRIED','{\n\"trail_name\": \"Scenic Kanab\",\n\"distance\": \">60 mi\",\n\"best_season\": \"May-September\",\n\"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n\"elevation\": \"7835 feet\",\n\"latitude\": null,\n\"longitude\": null,\n\"accessibility\": \"No\",\n\"pets_allowed\": null,\n\"reservation_required\": null,\n\"average_hours_to_hike\": null,\n\"vehicle\": \"ATV\",\n\"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 60 miles north on Scenic Highway 89. Turn right on Scenic Byway 12. Slow down and enjoy the beauty of Red Canyon. Travel about 13.5 miles and just past mile post 10 turn right (road #87). After 7 miles, you will see a sign for Tropic Reservoir and Kings Creek Campground. Turn right and continue around the reservoir for half a mile to the parking area for the Panguitch trailhead. If you have time, take the 3-mile ATV trail to Ruby\'s Inn for breakfast, lunch, or dinner.\",\n\"description\": \"Tropic Reservoir is a small scenic lake situated in the Dixie National Forest of Southern Utah. It is very popular for fishing, boating, ATV riding, mountain biking, hiking, and snowmobiling. The Panguitch Trail offers views of the Pink Cliffs on the Grand Staircase National Forest and Kaibab Plateau. Kings Creek Campground has a paved boat ramp suitable for small boats and kayaks. Fishing for brown, brook, rainbow, and cutthroat trout is great from the shore or boat. An ATV ride west from the campground and over the beautiful mountain will get you to Hatch. Riding the trails south and east will take you to spectacular orange hoodoos just outside Bryce Canyon, with breathtaking scenery and excellent hiking opportunities.\",\n\"description_html\": \"Tropic Reservoir is a small scenic lake situated in the Dixie National Forest of Southern Utah. It is very popular for fishing, boating, ATV riding, mountain biking, hiking, and snowmobiling. The Panguitch Trail offers views of the Pink Cliffs on the Grand Staircase National Forest and Kaibab Plateau. Kings Creek Campground has a paved boat ramp suitable for small boats and kayaks. Fishing for brown, brook, rainbow, and cutthroat trout is great from the shore or boat. An ATV ride west from the campground and over the beautiful mountain will get you to Hatch. Riding the trails south and east will take you to spectacular orange hoodoos just outside Bryce Canyon, with breathtaking scenery and excellent hiking opportunities.\",\n\"additional_info\": \"Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads can be impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas. Please do not remove artifacts or deface rock art. Leave no trace of your visit. Phone: 435.644.5033\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:45','2023-09-22 02:45:42'),(54,'trails',309,'WAHWEAP HOODOOS\nHIKE\nD\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nVEHICLE:\nKANAB 55 mi\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nWAHWEAP HOODOOS\nA DAY\nDIFFICULTY: DEPENDING ON WEATHER\nEASE TO HARD\nELEVATION:\nGETTING THERE\n• From Kanab, take HWY 89 east, approximately 55 miles to Big Water.\n• Turn left on the Ethan Allen Road, between mile 7 and 6, across the highway from the\nGrand Slaircuse Escalante Visitor Center.\n• Follow Ethan Allen past several streats to a \"T\" in the road. Turn left at the \"T\" and\nbypass the old softbell feld on the left side of the gravel rond. Follow this road pas two\nfish hatcheries.\n46\nlowers of Silence\n37 09.749 HOODOO\n111 42.708\nHOODOO\nYES\nSPRING FALL\nHIKE ONLY\n9 MILES ROUND TRIP\nContinue north past the corral which is 3 miles from HWY 89. 2 wheel drive vehicles,\npark on the right side of the road just before the creek.\n• Start your hike here by hiking up Wohweap Creek.\nHigh clearance 4-wheel drive vehicles can continue past the corral, crossing\nWahweap Creek, and ther continua 3 miles. The trail begins ir Wahweap Creek just\nbelow the 4WD parking.\n• Hike north up Wahweap Creek:\nDISCLAIMER:\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local roed and\nweather conditions, Stoy on designcted roads. Dirt roads are impassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivole Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water,\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in mos greas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock art\'. Lecve no trace of\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nyou visit so others may enjoy.\"\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 S 100 E\nCOYOTE CREEK\n102\nCX1\n(89)\nTrailhead 4WD parking PARK HERE WITH 4WD (P)\n3706.769\n111 41,309\n4042 FEET\nSLOT CANYONS\nREEK\nNIPPLE CREEK\nHoodoos and balancing rocks are a\nnatural wonder to see. Some describe\nthese as goblins, ghosts or toadstocals.\nEnjoy the unique geology of the sun\nscorchod lands of the Southwest. The\ncap of the hoodoo. a Dakota Sandstone\nthat was a beach of an incoming\nseaway, is 100 million years old.\nThe post is Entrade Sandstone that ist\n160 million years old. The headeas\ncome in many shapes and sizes and con\nbe reached by taking a leisurely, easily\nnovigaled hike through Wahweup Creek\nThere are a few little slot canyons along\nWahweap, Creek that you could also\nexplore. The Piutos believed that the\nhoodoos are the remnants of people\nwho were turned to pane. Caze up\ninto these cloves, in the norly morning\nand you will see why.\nP PARK HERE WITH 2WD\nNILE\nFISH HATCHERY\nO\nBIG WATER\nGSENMA\nVISITOR CENTER\nFISH HATCHERY\nGRAVEL PIT\nO\nLIFE\nADVENTURE MAP tread lightly! UTAH\n* O *\nCLEVATED\nGRAVEL PIT\nUT,\nAPP\nKANAB\nMODICILLUNSPOILED\nN\n1457\nKANAB\nKUGLY LOSPOLE','{\n   \"trail_name\": \"Wahweap Hoodoos\",\n   \"distance\": \"9 miles round trip\",\n   \"best_season\": \"Spring, Fall\",\n   \"difficulty\": \"Depends on weather, Easy to Hard\",\n   \"elevation\": \"4042 feet\",\n   \"latitude\": \"37.162485\",\n   \"longitude\": \"-111.711799\",\n   \"accessibility\": \"Yes\",\n   \"pets_allowed\": \"No\",\n   \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n   \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"A Day\",\n   \"vehicle\": \"2-wheel drive vehicles can park on the right side of the road just before the creek. High clearance 4-wheel drive vehicles can continue past the corral, crossing Wahweap Creek and park at the 4WD parking area just below the trailhead.\",\n   \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, take HWY 89 east for approximately 55 miles to Big Water. Turn left on the Ethan Allen Road between mile 7 and 6, across the highway from the Grand Staircase-Escalante Visitor Center. Follow Ethan Allen Road until you reach a T-junction and turn left. Continue past the corral for 3 miles. 2WD vehicles should park on the right side of the road. The trailhead starts by hiking up Wahweap Creek.\",\n   \"description\": \"Wahweap Hoodoos is a scenic hike located near Big Water, Utah. The trail takes hikers through Wahweap Creek, offering views of unique geological formations, including hoodoos and balancing rocks. The hike is approximately 9 miles round trip and can be completed in a day. The difficulty level of the hike varies depending on weather conditions, ranging from easy to hard. The best seasons to hike are spring and fall when the weather is mild. Pets are not allowed on the trail. It is recommended to bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies for a wilderness experience.\",\n   \"description_html\": \"<p>Wahweap Hoodoos is a scenic hike located near Big Water, Utah. The trail takes hikers through Wahweap Creek, offering views of unique geological formations, including hoodoos and balancing rocks. The hike is approximately 9 miles round trip and can be completed in a day. The difficulty level of the hike varies depending on weather conditions, ranging from easy to hard. The best seasons to hike are spring and fall when the weather is mild. Pets are not allowed on the trail. It is recommended to bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies for a wilderness experience.</p>\",\n   \"additional_info\": \"Always check local road and weather conditions before starting the hike. Stay on designated roads and avoid dirt roads when wet. Respect private property and do not remove artifacts or deface rock art. Leave no trace and carry out all trash. Cell phone service is limited in the area.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:55','2023-09-22 0